A||||10||william_thorniii||Anyone Interested in a Street Dreams Brake Kit w/ Drop Spindles?||||1||
Z||000000||william_thorniii||11-10-2000||08:29 AM||||Friends,<P>I received a message from Ross at Streat Dreams last night.  The message is attached below.  <P>From:  "Street Dreams" &lt;strdream@iserv.net&gt; Save Address - Block Sender <BR> Add Street Dreams to your Messenger contact list. <BR>To:  "WILLIAM THORN, III" &lt;william_thorniii@hotmail.com&gt; Save Address <BR>Subject:  I have an idea <BR>Date:  Thu, 9 Nov 2000 19:53:49 -0500 <BR>Reply Reply All Forward  Delete  Previous Next  Close <P>William<P>You may not believe this but I haven't even filled the current requests<BR>for<BR>spindles yet and I have an idea on an improvement that I could make. <BR>Would<BR>you<BR>please put the word out with your group about the change and get some<BR>feed-back for me?  I would like to know if it would be worth the expense<BR>of<BR>development.<P>   What I am thinking of doing is to modify the casting pattern to<BR>provide a<BR>bigger spindle and than turn it to fit the bearings and rotor for the<BR>1985<BR>Monte<BR>Carlo brakes.<P>I know that you can get the bigger brakes in a kit now.  The advantage<BR>here<BR>would be the bigger bearings to provide a better loading when using<BR>bigger<BR>wheels<BR>and tires.  With this upgrade kit I would be providing everything in one<BR>package, the beefier two inch drop spindle, rotors, new bearings, seals,<BR>calipers and axle cap.<P>I will still offer the plain 2 inch drop spindle that works with all the<BR>existing hardware on the '84-'87 Fiero.  Which by the way I have 9 more<BR>prs<BR>of<P>Our present idea for the new rotor would be to use a stock rotor that<BR>can<BR>fit the larger bearings, but it has a 5 bolt, 4 3/4" bolt circle.  This<BR>may<BR>make it easier to find wheels for the Fiero but the downside is that<BR>those<BR>wanting to keep their present wheels would need to buy a wheel adaptor.<P>Do you think there is a big enough market for this idea?<P>Thanks for your input.<P>Ross<P>Well... There you have it.  Give him your feedback.  Please be honest, he already has a large amount of money that he has not yet recovered from his spindle conquest.  <P>Along the spindle note... I have received mine and am very happy with them.  Don't ask for pictures yet though... the car is several months away from seeing the floor.  I promise that the first time I drop her, you will all be the first to see her.  <P>Thanks,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000001||Oreif||11-10-2000||08:58 AM||||Sounds nice about the brakes. But I am going to Email him and ask if he can change the bolt pattern on the rear to match the front. Otherwise finding wheels that look the same with the different pattern could prove to be a pain. Wheel adatpter could add unwanted offset depending on the wheels available.||||reg||1
Z||000002||william_thorniii||11-10-2000||09:09 AM||||Orief,<P>That was one of my conearns as well.  However, for those interested in handling on our 84-87's... the additional offset will only decrease scrub radius and is therefore welcomed on my vehicle.  Good points though.<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
Z||000003||pollock||11-10-2000||10:07 AM||||This is what i've been wanting.  I hate the stupid grand am "upgrade" because it's not much of an upgrade, and you can't put an ebrake on the back.  The whole point of using the grand am brakes is to keep your wheels, but the aftermarket wheel selection for a 5 on 100mm bolt pattern SUCKS.  I'd love to have some Z28SS wheels on my car.  <P>I guess if you used this guy's stuff you'd want to put some camaro disks on the back.  <P>In all honesty, i'm not buying any.... just sharing my thoughts on the matter.||||reg||1
Z||000004||lou_dias||11-10-2000||12:39 PM||||Could this be what I've been waiting for?  will this allow me to put the late 80's GTA Firebird Wheels 16x8 that look like the Fiero Gt's wheels on all 4 corners?||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||11-10-2000||01:15 PM||||Friends,<P>Ross can get just about any bolt spacing that you want.  So yes and yes... your dreams could be realized with this kit. <P>William T. Thorn, III  ||||reg||14
Z||000006||88formula||11-11-2000||04:00 PM||||What about drop spindles for 88 fiero's?||||reg||1
Z||000007||gixxer||11-11-2000||05:14 PM||||To ThornIII; if you don't like the scrub radius, change the wheel offset.<BR>The bump kickback problem is mostly in the spindle LENGTH anyway. That's why a complete redesign was necessary for the '88s. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||strdream||11-11-2000||06:24 PM||||Hey It's me, Ross. Thanks for the info. I think that if I can make the Fiero wheel fit on the bigger hub I will go for the up-grade and redrill the bolt pattern. Second best option will be to look into redrilling a set of rear rotors to match the 4.75 pattern on the front ones.  I wish I could get my hands on a set of '88 spindles for a few days to see if there is somthing I can adapt or machine differantly to make the drop spindles work on the '88. Any offers?<P>Thanks again<BR>Ross<P>P.S.  new to the forum||||reg||1
Z||000009||Terrybogin||11-11-2000||06:43 PM||||Somebody with an 88 parts car PLEASE get them to this guy!||||reg||1
Z||000010||william_thorniii||11-13-2000||08:33 AM||||Gixxer,<P>One can only add so much in offset.  Some manufacturers are now offering 45-50 mm offsets but they are rare.  Just about all of 50mm is required to eliminate the scrub radius.  <P>strdream,<P>I would either contact the Fiero Shop or the Fiero Factory.  Either of them should have a pair and they might be able to let you borrow them or something.  Where's Terry?  If you dig around in the mall section you should find a response from Terry at the Fiero Shop to about every part inquiry.  <P>Later,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||14
